## Debug Sequence (condensed)

Eight theories ruled out in order before reaching root cause:

| # | Theory | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | RBAC/manifest misconfiguration | ✅ Correct, unchanged since April 25 |
| 2 | Vault Agent sidecar corrupting SA token mount | ✅ Token present, valid JWT, correct identity |
| 3 | Stale pod state (31 days, 128 restarts) | ✅ Deleted pod, new pod crashed identically |
| 4 | RBAC binding deleted / API server auth broken | ✅ `kubectl create token` + manual `kubectl get nodes --token=...` → all 6 nodes returned successfully |
| 5 | Missing env vars (`KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST/PORT`) | ✅ All present and correct |
| 6 | Python client config not loading | ✅ `load_incluster_config()` loaded host/token/CA correctly — yet the API call still failed |
| 7 | Network path / `Bearer` vs `bearer` case sensitivity | ✅ Raw `requests` library call with same token → **200 OK**. Proved network + token + RBAC all fine; problem was isolated to the kubernetes Python library specifically |
| 8 | Explicit `Configuration` object passing | ✅ Still failed identically |

**Breakthrough** — traced the auth chain inside the library itself:
```python
api.update_params_for_auth(h, [], ['BearerToken'])  # → {} EMPTY
api.configuration.auth_settings()                    # → {} EMPTY, no 'BearerToken' key
```
The token was present in `api_key`, but the auth scheme definitions that map `api_key` → HTTP `Authorization` header were gone. **Root cause located inside `auth_settings()`.**

### Image/version investigation

| | Broken (4 days old at time of incident) | Working reference |
|---|---|---|
| **kubernetes** | **36.0.0** | **35.0.0** |
| urllib3 | 2.7.0 | 2.6.3 |

Confirmed via `ctr run` pulling both image digests directly and printing library versions inside each. Git history on the prod manifests/code showed no changes since April 25 — confirming the regression was purely a dependency version bump introduced by an unpinned `pip install kubernetes` at image build time, not a code change.

**Trigger**: a dev→prod merge 4 days prior touched no Python/k8s files, but still triggered a CI rebuild of `:latest`. The Dockerfile had no version pin, so the rebuild silently grabbed `kubernetes==36.0.0`. Prod server was powered off for those 4 days, so the broken image only got scheduled and exercised once the server was started — at which point CrashLoopBackOff began immediately.

