## Fix Implemented

### 1. Dockerfile — pinned all previously-floating dependencies

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.11-slim

# Pin kubectl explicitly — matches cluster v1.35.3. Do NOT use stable.txt (unpinned = surprise upgrades)
ARG KUBECTL_VERSION=v1.35.3

RUN apt-get update && \
    apt-get install -y curl ca-certificates iputils-ping traceroute dnsutils vim-tiny openssh-client jq netcat-openbsd procps && \
    curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/${KUBECTL_VERSION}/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl" && \
    chmod +x kubectl && \
    mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/ && \
    apt-get clean && \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Pinned. kubernetes 36.0.0 broke auth_settings()/bearer token auth in load_incluster_config().
# Stay on 35.x until a v36 migration is done deliberately and tested.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir \
    kubernetes==35.0.0 \
    urllib3==2.6.3 \
    requests==2.32.3 \
    proxmoxer==2.1.0

CMD ["python", "/scripts/remediation.py"]
```

This closes both the original bug (unpinned `kubernetes`) and a second, identical-shape risk found in the same file (unpinned `kubectl` via `dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt`).

### 2. Rebuild, push, reconcile

- Rebuilt image from the pinned Dockerfile, pushed to `ghcr.io/mohamedsabry-dev/remediation`
- Flux auto-reconciled the deployment to the new image automatically (Flux-managed via `kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io`)
- The in-flight crashed pod was deleted manually to force immediate pull of the corrected image rather than waiting through the existing restart backoff window

### 3. Verified

Pod came up clean on the rebuilt image, health checks against all 6 nodes (3 masters, 3 workers) succeeded, no further 403s, no further crash loop.

